The existence of a vector $\mathbf{v}$ such that $\{\mathbf{v}, T(\mathbf{v}), \ldots, T^{n-1}(\mathbf{v})\}$ is linearly independent implies that $T$ has a cyclic vector. This means the minimal polynomial of $T$ equals its characteristic polynomial, making $T$ non-derogatory. For non-derogatory matrices, each eigenvalue has geometric multiplicity 1, meaning the eigenspace for each eigenvalue is one-dimensional. Thus, any eigenvector corresponding to an eigenvalue is unique up to scalar multiplication.

